%% Handing a cluster's deployment over to Polar's own git server (cluster-bootstrap.md, "Handing off to Polar's git server"). All designed, nothing
%% built. Who makes the copy, and how Flux is identified to the server, are open.
sequenceDiagram
    autonumber
    actor Op as Operator
    participant Boot as Bootstrap git server (polar-deploy)
    participant Flux as Flux
    participant Git as Polar's git server
    participant Cfg as Configuration agent
    participant Att as Attestation agent

    rect rgb(235, 219, 178)
    Note over Op,Flux: DESIGNED: the bootstrap, as it is today. Polar's git server does not exist yet
    Op->>Boot: commit the layers, signed by the pipeline certificate
    Flux->>Boot: read them, and apply them
    Flux->>Git: Polar's git server comes up, among the layers
    end

    rect rgb(235, 219, 178)
    Note over Boot,Git: DESIGNED: the handoff, once
    Boot-->>Git: the signed history, copied to deploy-T. The server refuses a commit the pipeline did not sign
    Op->>Git: a signed commit: Flux's source is now Polar's git server
    Flux->>Git: read from here on
    Op->>Boot: remove it. Its history stays in mirror.git, off the cluster
    end

    rect rgb(235, 219, 178)
    Note over Op,Att: DESIGNED: at nominal
    Op->>Git: a new deployment: a commit the pipeline signed
    Git->>Git: verify the signature to the deployment CA, or refuse the push
    Flux->>Git: read it, and apply it
    Cfg->>Git: read it, as it reads polar-schedules
    Cfg-)Att: the deployment's facts, as signed configuration
    Att->>Att: verify them to the deployment CA, for this section alone
    end

    rect rgb(235, 219, 178)
    Note over Op,Git: DESIGNED: if a bad commit stops Polar's git server, running pods keep running
    Op->>Boot: bring it back, from mirror.git, and point Flux at it to apply the fix
    end
